            'body_en' => "Tell Brak sits where the routes between Anatolia, the Levant and southern Mesopotamia crossed the Khabur plain, and people have lived on it since about 6500 BC. The mound is the accumulated rubble of their houses: forty metres of mudbrick, ash and potsherds, the tallest tell in the region. Its ancient name, from the third millennium BC, was Nagar.\n\nWhat makes Brak extraordinary is its fourth millennium. By around 4200 BC the settlement had city walls and had spread into a lower town of some fifty-five hectares; at the same time, or slightly earlier than Uruk in the south, it was behaving like a city — with monumental buildings, a street paved with potsherds, mass-produced pottery and, at Tell Majnuna just beyond the mound, mass graves of the dead of some conflict. Urban life, it turned out, did not have a single cradle.\n\nThe mound was first dug in 1937 by Max Mallowan, with his wife Agatha Christie keeping the dig house and photographing the finds, and then for more than thirty seasons between 1976 and 2011 by teams led by David and Joan Oates and their successors — until the war in Syria stopped the work.",
